// Phase kickback: the mechanism behind most quantum algorithms.
//
// Concept
//   A controlled gate whose target is already an eigenstate of the controlled
//   operation does not change the target at all. Instead the eigenvalue is fed
//   back onto the *control* as a phase, where a Hadamard can turn it into a
//   measurable bit.
//
// Expected quantum behaviour
//   `q[1]` is prepared in |-> = (|0> - |1>)/sqrt(2), which is an eigenstate of
//   X with eigenvalue -1. (Note: of X, not of Z - the Z eigenstates are the
//   boring |0> and |1>.) The controlled-X then multiplies the |1> branch of
//   `q[0]` by -1 while leaving `q[1]` exactly where it was. The final H on
//   `q[0]` converts that relative phase into a definite outcome.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   `c` is 1 in 100% of shots, and `q[1]` is still in |-> at the end.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   Before the CX the state is |+>|-> = (1/sqrt2)(|0> + |1>) tensor |->.
//   Since X|-> = -|->, the CX sends this to
//
//     (1/sqrt2)(|0>|-> - |1>|->) = |-> tensor |->
//
//   which is still a *product* state: the qubits are not entangled, the phase
//   simply moved to the control. H then maps |-> to |1>.
//
//   This is exactly the step that makes Deutsch-Jozsa and phase estimation
//   work; `deutsch-jozsa.qf` is the same idea with a two-bit oracle.
//
// @expect qubits=2 clbits=1 gates=5
// @expect-outcome 1

qubit[2] q;
bit c;

// Put the target into the X eigenstate |->, whose eigenvalue is -1.
x q[1];
h q[1];

h q[0];
cx q[0], q[1];
h q[0];

measure q[0] -> c;
